{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-49005" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039985/assets/5e232394bb3f520386068110e42887faa288c31077a660570494ccc5a85b0486.png)\n\nGiven the graph of $y=f(x)$ above, which of the following statements is true?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039985/state.json" "file_sha256" "c62ea29a0513b9596016a33131143a5f341b024e55f9a0d9cff036c6456b0b0b"} {"question" "q-49005" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "From the graph, we see that\n\n- The slope of the tangent at $x=-1$ is zero, so ${f}^{′}(-1)=0.$\n\n- The slope of the tangent at $x=0$ is negative, so ${f}^{′}(0)<0.$\n\n- The slope of the tangent at $x=1$ is positive, so ${f}^{′}(1)>0.$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(0)<{f}^{′}(-1)<{f}^{′}(1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039985/state.json" "file_sha256" "c62ea29a0513b9596016a33131143a5f341b024e55f9a0d9cff036c6456b0b0b"} {"question" "q-49005" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039985/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c47aa4176a24b2cb379e668df1091baafed209130026766e3b170a021e6cf4dc"} {"question" "q-49005" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039985/state.json" "file_sha256" "c62ea29a0513b9596016a33131143a5f341b024e55f9a0d9cff036c6456b0b0b"} {"question" "q-49005" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{f}^{′}(-1)<{f}^{′}(0)<{f}^{′}(1)"] ["math" "{f}^{′}(-1)<{f}^{′}(1)<{f}^{′}(0)"] ["math" "{f}^{′}(0)<{f}^{′}(-1)<{f}^{′}(1)"] ["math" "{f}^{′}(0)<{f}^{′}(1)<{f}^{′}(-1)"] ["math" "{f}^{′}(1)<{f}^{′}(-1)<{f}^{′}(0)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039985/state.json" "file_sha256" "c62ea29a0513b9596016a33131143a5f341b024e55f9a0d9cff036c6456b0b0b"} {"question" "q-49005" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{f}^{′}(0)<{f}^{′}(-1)<{f}^{′}(1)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039985/state.json" "file_sha256" "c62ea29a0513b9596016a33131143a5f341b024e55f9a0d9cff036c6456b0b0b"} {"question" "q-132687" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039985/assets/37c80a68fc1ee7dd534c795a7cfae13e44f63b09df4f2e948b29acbd1fb7ff06.png)\n\nConsider the graph of $y=f(x)$ above. Which of the following statements is true?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039985/state.json" "file_sha256" "c62ea29a0513b9596016a33131143a5f341b024e55f9a0d9cff036c6456b0b0b"} {"question" "q-132687" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "From the graph, we see that\n\n- The slope of the tangent at $x=-4$ is positive, so ${f}^{′}(-4)>0.$\n\n- The slope of the tangent at $x=-3$ is zero, so ${f}^{′}(-3)=0.$\n\n- The slope of the tangent at $x=-1$ is negative, so ${f}^{′}(-1)<0.$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(-1)<{f}^{′}(-3)<{f}^{′}(-4).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039985/state.json" "file_sha256" "c62ea29a0513b9596016a33131143a5f341b024e55f9a0d9cff036c6456b0b0b"} {"question" "q-132687" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039985/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c47aa4176a24b2cb379e668df1091baafed209130026766e3b170a021e6cf4dc"} {"question" "q-132687" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039985/state.json" "file_sha256" "c62ea29a0513b9596016a33131143a5f341b024e55f9a0d9cff036c6456b0b0b"} {"question" "q-132687" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{f}^{′}(-1)<{f}^{′}(-3)<{f}^{′}(-4)"] ["math" "{f}^{′}(-3)<{f}^{′}(-1)<{f}^{′}(-4)"] ["math" "{f}^{′}(-3)<{f}^{′}(-4)<{f}^{′}(-1)"] ["math" "{f}^{′}(-4)<{f}^{′}(-1)<{f}^{′}(-3)"] ["math" "{f}^{′}(-4)<{f}^{′}(-3)<{f}^{′}(-1)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039985/state.json" "file_sha256" "c62ea29a0513b9596016a33131143a5f341b024e55f9a0d9cff036c6456b0b0b"} {"question" "q-132687" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{f}^{′}(-1)<{f}^{′}(-3)<{f}^{′}(-4)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039985/state.json" "file_sha256" "c62ea29a0513b9596016a33131143a5f341b024e55f9a0d9cff036c6456b0b0b"} {"question" "q-316736" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039985/assets/6c3a45ac200d1f65c326354297e6e03b5347b4d4ec3fbb2a99bb7cde98376db8.png)\n\nFind the values of $x$ for which the function $g(x)=\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)}{x}$ (plotted above) is strictly decreasing.\n\n$x\\in (${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039985/state.json" "file_sha256" "c62ea29a0513b9596016a33131143a5f341b024e55f9a0d9cff036c6456b0b0b"} {"question" "q-316736" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The function is strictly decreasing where ${g}^{′}(x)<0.$ Differentiating $g(x)$ using the quotient rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{2}{3x}\\cdot 3)x-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n\\frac{2(1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))}{{x}^{2}}<0.\n$$\n\nSince $x>0$ is the domain of $g$ and ${x}^{2}>0$ for $x>0,$ the denominator is positive; thus this reduces to\n\n$$\n1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)<0.\n$$\n\nSolving $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)=1$ gives the critical point\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{e}{3}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, this critical point is a maximum. The slope of the tangent lines is positive to the left of the critical point and negative to the right of the critical point. \n\nSo, we summarize the sign of the derivative in the following table:\n\n$$\n\\left.\\begin{aligned}x & (0,\\frac{e}{3}) & \\frac{e}{3} & (\\frac{e}{3},\\infty ) \\\\ g & ↗ & \\text{max} & ↘ \\\\ {g}^{′} & + & 0 & -\\end{aligned}\\right|\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the function is strictly decreasing on the interval $(\\frac{e}{3},\\infty ).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039985/state.json" "file_sha256" "c62ea29a0513b9596016a33131143a5f341b024e55f9a0d9cff036c6456b0b0b"} {"question" "q-316736" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039985/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c47aa4176a24b2cb379e668df1091baafed209130026766e3b170a021e6cf4dc"} {"question" "q-316736" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039985/state.json" "file_sha256" "c62ea29a0513b9596016a33131143a5f341b024e55f9a0d9cff036c6456b0b0b"} {"question" "q-316736" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{e}{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039985/state.json" "file_sha256" "c62ea29a0513b9596016a33131143a5f341b024e55f9a0d9cff036c6456b0b0b"} {"question" "q-316736" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039985/state.json" "file_sha256" "c62ea29a0513b9596016a33131143a5f341b024e55f9a0d9cff036c6456b0b0b"} {"question" "q-316736" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\infty"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039985/state.json" "file_sha256" "c62ea29a0513b9596016a33131143a5f341b024e55f9a0d9cff036c6456b0b0b"} {"question" "q-316787" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the values of $x$ for which the function $g(x)=({x}^{2}-3){e}^{x}$ is strictly increasing.\n\n$x<$\n{{field-1}} $\\,$ and $\\,x>$\n{{field-2}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039985/state.json" "file_sha256" "c62ea29a0513b9596016a33131143a5f341b024e55f9a0d9cff036c6456b0b0b"} {"question" "q-316787" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The function is strictly increasing where ${g}^{′}(x)>0.$ Differentiating $g(x)$ using the product rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{2}-3){e}^{x}+2x\\,{e}^{x} \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+2x-3){e}^{x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n({x}^{2}+2x-3){e}^{x}>0.\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{x}>0$ for all real $x,$ this reduces to\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2x-3>0.\n$$\n\nThe above inequality factors as\n\n$$\n(x+3)(x-1)>0.\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y=(x+3)(x-1)$ has roots at $x=-3$ and $x=1,$ as shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039985/assets/9b847390fb6de568144d309c45fb8c904475bdde79a46a90ec0fd4426a71b57b.png)\n\nFor an upward-opening parabola, the product $(x+3)(x-1)$ is positive when $x<-3$ or $x>1.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the function is strictly increasing on the intervals \n\n$$\nx<-3\\,\\text{and}\\,x>1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039985/state.json" "file_sha256" "c62ea29a0513b9596016a33131143a5f341b024e55f9a0d9cff036c6456b0b0b"} {"question" "q-316787" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039985/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c47aa4176a24b2cb379e668df1091baafed209130026766e3b170a021e6cf4dc"} {"question" "q-316787" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039985/state.json" "file_sha256" "c62ea29a0513b9596016a33131143a5f341b024e55f9a0d9cff036c6456b0b0b"} {"question" "q-316787" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-3"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039985/state.json" "file_sha256" "c62ea29a0513b9596016a33131143a5f341b024e55f9a0d9cff036c6456b0b0b"} {"question" "q-316787" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039985/state.json" "file_sha256" "c62ea29a0513b9596016a33131143a5f341b024e55f9a0d9cff036c6456b0b0b"} {"question" "q-316787" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039985/state.json" "file_sha256" "c62ea29a0513b9596016a33131143a5f341b024e55f9a0d9cff036c6456b0b0b"} {"question" "q-343574" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{2}{5}\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}-\\frac{4}{3}\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}+2$ is defined for $x\\in (0,\\infty ),$ find the values of $x$ for which the function is strictly decreasing.\n\n$x\\in (${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039985/state.json" "file_sha256" "c62ea29a0513b9596016a33131143a5f341b024e55f9a0d9cff036c6456b0b0b"} {"question" "q-343574" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We first write $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{2}{5}{x}^{5/2}-\\frac{4}{3}{x}^{3/2}+2.\n$$\n\nThe function is strictly decreasing where ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$ Differentiating $f(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}{x}^{3/2}-\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}{x}^{1/2} \\\\  & ={x}^{3/2}-2{x}^{1/2} \\\\  & ={x}^{1/2}(x-2) \\\\  & =(x-2)\\sqrt{x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n(x-2)\\sqrt{x}<0.\n$$\n\nThe expression $\\sqrt{x}$ is always positive. So, this reduces to\n\n$$\nx-2<0.\n$$\n\nSolving this inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2 & <0 \\\\ x & <2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the function is restricted to $x\\in (0,\\infty ),$ we conclude that $f(x)$ is strictly decreasing on the interval $x\\in (0,2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039985/state.json" "file_sha256" "c62ea29a0513b9596016a33131143a5f341b024e55f9a0d9cff036c6456b0b0b"} {"question" "q-343574" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039985/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c47aa4176a24b2cb379e668df1091baafed209130026766e3b170a021e6cf4dc"} {"question" "q-343574" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039985/state.json" "file_sha256" "c62ea29a0513b9596016a33131143a5f341b024e55f9a0d9cff036c6456b0b0b"} {"question" "q-343574" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039985/state.json" "file_sha256" "c62ea29a0513b9596016a33131143a5f341b024e55f9a0d9cff036c6456b0b0b"} {"question" "q-343574" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039985/state.json" "file_sha256" "c62ea29a0513b9596016a33131143a5f341b024e55f9a0d9cff036c6456b0b0b"} {"question" "q-343574" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039985/state.json" "file_sha256" "c62ea29a0513b9596016a33131143a5f341b024e55f9a0d9cff036c6456b0b0b"}] "usage" {"q-49005" []} "basis" 1400 "retractions" [[17592186106290 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1359/Source/Images/q-49005.png)\n\nGiven the graph of $y = f(x)$ above, which of the following statements is true?"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186104250 17592186104251 17592186104252 17592186104253 17592186104254] "immutable_fields" [[17592186104249 :answer-field/correct] [17592186104249 :answe